# {{.BinaryName}}

An epack collector built with the [Component SDK](https://github.com/locktivity/epack).

See [docs/](docs/) for usage documentation.

## Layout

| Path | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `cmd/{{.BinaryName}}/main.go` | The entrypoint, which runs the handler |
| `internal/collector/handler.go` | The collector's spec and handler |
| `internal/collector/collector.go` | The collection, which builds the artifact |
| `internal/collector/gaps.go` | Records each value the collector leaves out, and why |
| `internal/{{.VendorPackage}}/client.go` | The API client, with one named operation per call |
| `docs/schema/v1.0.0.json` | The artifact's JSON Schema, with the `x-epack` annotations the output checks read |

## Development

```bash
# Build
go build -o {{.BinaryName}} {{.MainPackage}}

# Test capabilities
./{{.BinaryName}} --capabilities

# Run locally (with epack SDK)
epack sdk run ./{{.BinaryName}}

# Watch mode: auto-rebuild on file changes
epack sdk run --watch .
```

## Testing

```bash
go test -race ./...
```

`internal/collector/collectortest_test.go` runs the collector against canned API responses: an organization written by hand in `fixtures_test.go`, and one with no users. It then makes each API call fail in turn, with errors such as a 403, a timeout, or a malformed body, and checks that each value the collector could not read is left out with a gap. It also checks the output against `docs/schema/v1.0.0.json` at every collection level, and checks that the token never reaches another host or any output. Add a scenario for each state of an organization the collector has to handle.

### Recording a scenario

`make record` runs the collector once against a real organization and writes the API's responses to `internal/collector/testdata/recordings/clean.json`, with the token, the organization's name, emails, and IP addresses scrubbed:

```bash
RECORD_ORGANIZATION=your-org {{.TokenEnv}}=your-token make record
```

To test against the recording, load it with `collectortest.LoadScenario` in place of the scenario in `fixtures_test.go`.

### Conformance

```bash
make conformance
```

This runs the conformance harness at the epack version `go.mod` pins, with `testdata/conformance.json` as the config and a canary value in each variable `CONFORMANCE_SECRETS` lists in the Makefile. Keep the config free of secrets. Conformance fails if any output prints a canary, and checks that a rejected token exits 3.

## CI

`.github/workflows/ci.yaml` calls epack's shared collector workflow, which runs the tests, lint, release builds, conformance, and govulncheck. On a pull request it also compares each scenario's output with the output of the latest release.

## Release

Tag a version to trigger the release workflow:

```bash
git tag v0.1.0
git push origin v0.1.0
```

The GitHub Action will:
1. Run CI
2. Build multi-platform binaries (linux/darwin, amd64/arm64)
3. Generate [SLSA Level 3](https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/levels#build-l3) provenance attestations
4. Publish to GitHub Releases

All release binaries include cryptographic provenance that proves they were built from this repository's CI pipeline.

